WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:04.720
Mike Wolfe and Frank Fritz struck gold with
their hit History Channel series American

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:08.480
Pickers, which follows the besties around
the country as they search for treasure in

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.900
America's garages, basements, and outbuildings.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.520
Not every trip is a major payday, but sometimes
the boys get lucky.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:19.200
Here are some of the most expensive items
ever discovered on American Pickers.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:24.300
Fritz and Wolfe have a special affinity for
rare cars manufactured by companies no longer

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:24.840
in business.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:30.940
In this season 12 episode, they stumbled on
an exceptionally scarce Auburn Phaeton 653

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:35.200
from 1935, the last year of production on
that particular automobile.

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:41.399
They paid its owner $26,500 for the car, although
it cost them another $1,000 to ship it back

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.920
to American Pickers HQ, where they spent around
$10,000 to make it look and run as good as

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:47.420
new.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:54.540
It wound up with a value of $45,000, and after
subtracting the $37,500 investment, that makes

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:57.120
for a tidy profit of $7,500.

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:01.940
License plates today are made of metal, and
issued by state motor vehicle departments

00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:04.380
as a way to uniquely identify each vehicle.

00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:09.800
But when cars became commonplace more than
100 years ago, the government issued license

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:15.150
numbers, but not plates, meaning drivers or
car companies had make their own, and they

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:19.180
sometimes used unusual materials to do so,
such as embossed leather.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:24.500
Wolfe thought this leather beauty was pretty
peculiar, and paid a whopping $1,000 for the

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:25.420
privilege of ownership.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:29.000
He later sold it, and made a nice $200 on
the flip.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.540
American Pickers season three began with the
duo hitting the road for Bushkill Park, an

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:35.680
amusement park in Easton, Pennsylvania.

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.500
One of the oldest continuously operating amusement
parks in the country, it opened in 1902 but

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:43.120
mostly closed down in the 2000s.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:47.480
On a guided tour of the property they found
some whimsical and enticing sideshow banners

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.320
used to promote the park's circus events long,
long ago.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:54.880
Knowing they were worth something, the Pickers
walked away with a few for $700, but were

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.320
delighted when they later sold for $10,000.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:03.970
Royal Pioneer specialized in luxury motorbikes
until a factory fire limited the company's

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:08.080
lifetime production at around 500 and destroyed
the entire operation.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:13.700
That makes a Royal Pioneer motorcycle in any
condition rare — in fact, only four are

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:14.820
known to exist.

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:20.200
That's why Wolfe forked over $55,000 for the
skeleton of a 1910 specimen.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:24.900
Sure, it didn't look like much, but a little
elbow grease and he'd be sure to make a tidy

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:25.400
profit.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:29.840
After all, another restored Royal Pioneer
motorcycle had previously sold for nearly

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:31.280
$100,000.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:36.890
While swinging through New England in late
2013, the Pickers visited Holyoke, Massachusetts,

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.420
home of Paper City Brewery.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:42.950
Co-owners and brothers Jay and Jon Hebert
keep a nice collection of classic motorcycles

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.800
in the company's mill building, so Wolfe and
Fritz soon found themselves in motorcycle

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:47.300
heaven.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:51.980
Of the more than a dozen bikes on display,
the duo bought five altogether, including

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:53.600
a 1932 Harley-Davidson.

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:58.200
Total offering price of that loud and powerful
haul: $62,000.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:02.840
The guys didn't exactly know what to expect
in season 16 when they were approached by

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.680
a woman who promised that she had an interesting
and extensive collection of movie memorabilia.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.000
But they were blown away by what they saw.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.080
The collector was in possession of several
awesome pieces, including a prototype of an

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:18.520
officially licensed Yoda statue created in
the mid-'90s.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.540
Even a non-collector knows that a one-of-a-kind
Star Wars piece like this is going to be valuable,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:26.900
especially considering it's different than
the manufactured statue.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:32.260
Take home a Yoda the Pickers did, for the
relatively low price of $6,250.

00:03:33.140 --> 00:03:37.350
In the season seven premiere, the team spotted
what seemed at first glance to be an original

00:03:37.350 --> 00:03:40.540
Lionel model train set, and one in mint condition.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:45.400
They agreed to pay $8,000 for the whole shebang
and couldn't believe now lucky they were.

00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:49.540
That would turn out, unfortunately, to be
one of the most disappointing acquisitions

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:50.770
in American Pickers history.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.500
When they asked an auctioneer for an appraisal,
they learned the toy wasn't quite what they'd

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.000
thought.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:04:01.180
The train was not 100 percent original parts
— many had been replaced, significantly

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.540
reducing its overall value.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:08.660
Ultimately, the Pickers unloaded the train
set for $3,400, less than half of what they'd

00:04:08.670 --> 00:04:09.670
paid for it.

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:10.760
"I kind of want to throw up."

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.900
Sometimes Lady Luck is with Mike and Frank
during the filming of American Pickers, like

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.420
the time in season five when they found an
actual dinosaur bone.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:23.320
The guys also found fortune eleven seasons
later with something decidedly less prehistoric:

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.020
an Eisenhower-era motorcycle with an attached
sidecar.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.670
The guys managed to find a rare Zundapp RS
750, and paid out a not-insubstantial sum

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:35.740
of $10,000 to own it, plus $1,000 in shipping.

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.040
After spending some more cash to restore it,
a Georgia vintage motorcycle shop took it

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.380
off their hands for $18,000.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:48.020
In the middle of season 13 of American Pickers,
somehow, someway, the team discovered two

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:50.540
Nash-Healeys at an old AMC dealership.

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:56.360
Nash Motors only produced cars from 1951 to
1954, and high-end and classic car collectors

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.580
love them, so to find two of these sleek and
wonderful sports cars just hanging out and

00:05:00.580 --> 00:05:04.640
gathering dust is kind of unreal, so the guys
just had to make an offer.

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:10.560
$46,000 is a lot of money to spend on anything,
let alone two cars that hadn't hit the road

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.300
in decades.

00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:14.660
Yet in the end, as they often do, the Pickers
came out on top.

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:20.530
According to the Hagerty Price Guide, each
car is worth around $37,000, which means Mike

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:23.680
and Fritz basically got two Nash-Healeys for
the price of one.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:30.060
This episode from the show's seventeenth season
bore the cryptic title "The $90,000 Question,"

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:33.919
because that's roughly the amount the guys
consider spending on an unbelievable lot of

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:37.860
old motorcycles in various states of disrepair
on a property in Oregon.

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:43.120
There among the slowly rotting hunks of once-grand
metal sat a few choice picks, including a

00:05:43.120 --> 00:05:47.620
decades-old Ace four-cylinder number, one
of the rarest motorcycles in the world.

00:05:48.290 --> 00:05:51.920
It's not going to be cheap for Mike and Frank
to get it into roadworthy shape, but it was

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.260
just too good to pass up.

00:05:53.720 --> 00:05:58.779
Altogether, the guys pull the trigger and
actually hand over about $90,000 on the bikes

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.370
— a Pickers record.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.840
"If they want to come out and buy more stuff?

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:03.340
Absolutely."

